Personally, I would consider it to be "playing a different game" or at
least "playing a variant", which I guess is kind of different from
cheating as such.  (Unless you somehow concoct a way to make a public
server, such as NAO, cough up this information, which would indeed be
cheating outright; but that's probably impossible from the client
side.)  The game source is released under a license that allows
modifications, so making modifications is not per se cheating.  If you
boast about your performance while failing to mention that you're
playing a different game from everyone else, that would certainly be
dishonest...

> It gives you way more helpful info than just knowing appearance.

More to the point, BUC info for non-BUC identified items is very
clearly not supposed to be available in vanilla NetHack.  Item
appearances for items on remote tiles *is* at least partially
available in vanilla NetHack, and it is not at all clear that this is
a bug in the traditional sense.

It could arguably be considered a design flaw, since it creates
inconsistencies, some of which have been discussed upthread.
Nonetheless, we could argue all day about how the game *should* have
been designed, but it is clear that 3.4.3 *was* designed to disgorge
at least some information about item appearance for items on remote
tiles.  The ; command gets you quite a bit, and color has been
mentioned, and the tileset build (the official one) displays different
tiles for different things (which is particularly useful for monsters
but can also be handy for items).  I don't think what Vulture's Eye is
doing, as described in this thread, is a categorical departure from
the design of 3.4.3.  In making implementation decisions, it may in
certain instances draw the line (for what to reveal) in a slightly
different place, but that's nitpicking the finer points of detail on
something that, in the vanilla game, does not appear to have been made
sufficiently consistent to warrant such nitpicking.  In particular,
the differences between tty and tiles are at least as great as the
differences between the official tile build and Vulture's Eye (in
terms of what information is revealed; other differences, such as the
isometric presentation, are arguably a much bigger change, but that's
not what we were talking about here).

Vanilla NetHack is however more consistent about when you can know the
BUC status of an item.  In this regard, I am not aware of any
differences between the tty and tileset builds, for example, and being
blind has relatively little effect on it (unless you're blind _when_
you drop the items on an altar, of course, or fail to see a monster
wield a weapon and get it welded to his hand because you're blind, or
that sort of thing).  A patch that causes the UI to disgorge BUC
status (to non-Monks) for non-BUC-identified items would be a major
fundamental change to an important game mechanic.  This is not a small
UI-related issue.  It's a fundamental game-design issue.

Now, if you want to talk about a patch that causes the ; command or a
tile overlay to report the BUC status of formally BUC-identified items
(e.g., items that have previously been dropped on altars by the player
while not blind), then we could talk about whether that might possibly
be a UI feature and a convenience, comparable in power and scope to
some of the patches that NAO has incorporated.  Possibly.  (Having ;
and/or a tooltip report #name information would be a logical companion
feature.  Named monsters are already so reported, and, similarly,
messages about monster activity sometimes report BUC status when it is
known.)  This would be much more similar to the kind of "sky blue
potion" information we're talking about.  Either way we're talking
about presenting *available* information; it's just a question of how
conveniently you can avail yourself of it.  Do you have to actually
walk over to the item and stand on the same tile with it, or can you
just use farlook, or does the game just tell you in the first place?

Making the UI tell you BUC status for non-BUC-identified items would
be more comparable to making it tell you that the sky blue potion
happens to be a potion of confusion, when it hasn't actually been
identified as such yet, or having it tell you the enchantment level of
weapons when you haven't identified them yet.


